course: dictionary
lesson: 13482
title: Identifiable: easy to recognise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Identifiable means you can tell who or what something is. If you can identify something, you can recognise it or name it. «The lost coat was easily identifiable because it had a bright red patch.» The coat could be picked out and named, so identifiable means able to be recognised. Check by swapping in "easy to recognise" and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The word can also mean clear and separate. «The rash had an identifiable cause: a new soap.» Here identifiable means you can point to one definite cause. Look at the words around it to help you tell which meaning is being used.
explain: Look at the word's parts. Identify means to recognise or name. Add -able and it means able to be identified. Identifiable, more identifiable and easily identifiable all describe something you can recognise.
fact: Identifiable means able to be recognised or named.
fact: «Identifiable» can also mean clear and separate, as in «an identifiable pattern».
fact: «Personally identifiable information» tells strangers who or where you are, such as your name, school or address.
fact: Keep personally identifiable information private, especially online.
fact: Lost property should be identifiable, so write your name in it.
exercise: «The lost coat was easily identifiable by its bright red patch.» What does identifiable mean here? | Hard to find or see | Very heavy and warm | Torn and dirty | *Able to be recognised
exercise: «The rash had an identifiable cause: a new soap.» What does identifiable mean here? | Hidden and unknown | *Clear enough to be picked out and named | Sudden and painful | Very big and red
exercise: Which word means able to be recognised or named? Find and copy it from: «The bird was identifiable by its long tail.» | was | bird | tail | *identifiable
test: Ella writes her name inside her school bag so it is identifiable. Why does she do this? | Her bag is a present | So the bag is heavier | So it is a secret | *So anyone can recognise whose it is
test: «Never share your address or school name, because that is personally identifiable information.» What does personally identifiable information mean? | Pictures of your pets | A kind of food | *Details that tell strangers who or where you are | Your favourite colour
exam: In a science lesson, the children used a key to make the leaf identifiable. What does identifiable mean? | *Able to be named | Brand new and shiny | Very wet from the rain | Blown away by the wind
